  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nBroad mid/upper troughing continues across the north central\nCONUS, with the stationary front draped from roughly Kirksville\nthrough Kansas City as of 3 PM. Meanwhile, SPC mesoanalysis \nshows 1.8\" to 1.9\" PWATs in place across the entire CWA with \ndeep warm cloud depths along with 1500 to 2000 J/kg of ML CAPE \nand 40 to 50 knots of effective shear. Current radar imagery \nshows showers and a few benign thunderstorms across the region, \nbut with an approaching vort max and the low level jet to ramp \nup later this evening, showers and thunderstorms are expected to\nincrease in both coverage and intensity. Most recent CAM \nguidance suggests two primary focus areas for training \nthunderstorms and heavy rainfall - northeast Missouri (in the \ngeneral vicinity of Kirksville, which received heavy rainfall \nlast night and this morning) - and the Kansas City metro. The \n12z HREF had probability matched means of roughly 2 inches for \nportions of the KC metro, with the 18z HRRR continuing to \nsuggest a swath of 1 to 3\" possible for the KC metro. Thus, the \nflood watch has been expanded westward to include the KC metro. \nGiven the excellent set up for heavy rainfall (model guidance \nsuggests PWATs could increase over 2\" by tonight), would not be \nsurprised to see a few localized areas pick up 3+ inches of \nrainfall between tonight into Thursday morning.\n\nIn addition to the heavy rainfall threat, a few strong to \nsevere storms will also be possible, with damaging wind gusts \nlikely being the primary threat, and SPC expanded the Day 1 \nmarginal risk further to the north and west to include the KC \nmetro, as well. Showers and storms should exit the CWA to the \neast by mid to late Thursday morning. However, the stationary \nfront should remain over the region, and with additional \nperturbations translating through the mid/upper flow and ample \nmoisture remaining, another round of showers and thunderstorms \nwill be possible late Thursday night into Friday morning.\n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, the western ridge re-establishes\nitself, with a 597 dam H5 high developing over the Four Corners\nregion, yielding increasing mid level heights over our CWA. \nAfternoon highs should return to the 90s across the CWA for \nSaturday and Sunday, with max heat indices ranging from the \nupper 90s to the mid 100s, with generally dry weather likely.\nHot and humid conditions should continue into the start of next\nweek.",
